#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# count

#% text
# `count(xs, p)` gives the number of elements of the list `xs` that match the pattern `p`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A plain value as the pattern counts its occurrences: `count(xs, 0)`.

#% item
# Only the elements of `xs` are tested.

#% item
# To count by a test function, use `count_if`; to count every distinct element, `tally` or `counts`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
count([a, b, a, c, a], a)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
count([1, 2.5, 3, x], _: Int)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
count([f(1), g(2), f(3)], f(_))
#% output
#= 2

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A pattern with a condition:

#% cell
count([5, -2, 7, -1, 0], k_ when k > 0)
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# Strings:

#% cell
count(characters("mississippi"), "s")
#% output
#= 4

#% text
# The values of an association:

#% cell
count(assoc(a -> 1, b -> 2, c -> 1), 1)
#% output
#= 2

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# How often each letter occurs in a word, for one letter:

#% cell
count(characters("abracadabra"), "a")
#% output
#= 5

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `tally` counts every distinct element at once:

#% cell
tally(characters("mississippi"))
#% output
#= [["m", 1], ["i", 4], ["s", 4], ["p", 2]]

#% text
# `count_if` counts by a test function:

#% cell
count_if([5, -2, 7, -1, 0], x => x > 0)
#% output
#= 2

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A failure pattern is itself a failure and passes through the call instead of being matched; count failures with `count_if` and `is_failure`:

#% cell
results = [1.2, failure(not_converged, find_root), 0.7]
count(results, failure(___))
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
count_if(results, is_failure)
#% output
#= 1


#% text
# With the heads:

#% cell
count(1 + a + b^2, _, [-1], heads: true)
#% output
#= 6

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `count_if`, `tally`, `counts`, `cases`, `position`
